 <table width="100%" border="0" bgcolor="#FFFFFF" cellpadding="5">
  <tr>
    <td background="images/bg.gif"><font class=""><a href="modules.php?name=News&new_topic=0"></a></font><br><font class="testo_notizia" color="#000000"><b>Madonna and Paul McCartney are the HEADLINE ACTS.</b></font></td>
  </tr>
  <tr height="1">
    <td  background="images/riga.gif"></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td bgcolor="#FFFFFF">
	<div align=justify><font class="storytext"><P>From <STRONG>This Is London</STRONG>:</P>
<P><STRONG>Madonna and Sir Paul McCartney are to headline the Live 8 concert in Hyde Park this summer - and will make it the biggest official outdoor live music event in London history.</STRONG></P>
<P><SPAN class=bodytext><STRONG>More than 150,000 people</STRONG> will attend the landmark gig after the Government today pledged to expand the capacity of the site by 50 per cent. <BR><BR>The concert forms the centrepiece of a series of five live shows taking place simultaneously at <STRONG>Hyde Park in London</STRONG>, <STRONG>the Eiffel Tower in Paris</STRONG>, <STRONG>the Brandenburg Gate in Berlin</STRONG>, <STRONG>the Circus Maximus in Rome</STRONG> and a venue in <STRONG>Philadelphia</STRONG> on <STRONG>July 2</STRONG>. <BR><BR>They have been organised by <STRONG>Live Aid</STRONG> founders <STRONG>Bob Geldof</STRONG> and <STRONG>Midge Ure</STRONG> as part of a campaign to force the world’s richest nations to relieve poverty in the third world. <BR><BR>Culture Secretary Tessa Jowell today revealed that the attendance figure for <STRONG>Hyde Park</STRONG> would be increased by 50,000 especially for the 16-hour marathon music event. <BR><BR>She said: "<STRONG>The Government is 100 per cent behind Live8. We want it to be the biggest and best open-air concert that the capital has ever seen</STRONG>." She also promised to oversee a massive clear-up of the park immediately after the concert to restore it for visitors. <BR><BR><STRONG>Tickets for the Hyde Park concert will be made available through a special text lottery to be announced at 8am on Monday June 6 through TV, radio and newspapers. Giant screens will be erected across the UK for those not able to get a ticket</STRONG>. <BR><BR><STRONG>The five concerts have been planned to coincide with the G8 world leaders’ summit at Gleneagles in Edinburgh. The aim is not to raise cash through public donations, as with Live Aid in 1985, but to place international pressure on rich countries to act now as part of the Make Poverty History campaign</STRONG>. <BR><BR>U2, Sting and Oasis are also on the Hyde Park bill and Mariah Carey, OutKast, Mary J Blige and Linkin Park are expected to appear in the US. Full details of the <STRONG>Live8</STRONG> event, which starts at 2pm, are being unveiled today at a press conference hosted by Geldof. <BR><BR>Ms Jowell said: "The G8 Summit in Edinburgh, which follows the concert, offers an opportunity for the world’s developed nations to really make a difference - to do something positive for the Third World. Live8 will set the scene, and we are proud to be able to help make it happen." <BR><BR>Other stars expected to be confirmed on the <STRONG>Hyde Park</STRONG> bill are Robbie Williams, Duran Duran, Razorlight, Annie Lennox and Joss Stone - but despite rumours that the Spice Girls are to re-form, their name will not be announced today. <BR><BR>Others in the frame include Pink Floyd, Coldplay and Usher. <BR><BR>Jonathan Ross will present the London show and Radio DJ Jo Whiley will host the backstage coverage. <BR><BR>Destiny’s Child and Alicia Keys are also said to have been signed up for the US concert, which will be hosted by Graham Norton. At last week’s Ivor Novello Awards, Geldof said: "Once more into the breach. What started 20 years ago is coming to a political point in a few weeks. <BR><BR>"There’s more than a chance that the boys and girls with guitars will finally get to turn the world on its axis. What we do in the next five weeks is seriously, properly, historically, politically important." <BR><BR>He has reportedly asked the world’s most prominent figures, including the Pope, the Dalai Lama and Nelson Mandela to address crowds at the events in person or via satellite link-up. <BR><BR>It is estimated that around three billion people could tune to watch or listen to the event on TV and radio - twice that for Live Aid in 1985. All the UK commercial radio stations have agreed to air the show together, in a similar deal to the UK Radio Aid event earlier this year, which was organised to raise cash for the victims of the tsunami disaster. Giant screens will be put up across some 20 UK cities and towns for those who cannot get entry to the live event. <BR><BR>Meanwhile, Geldof is calling for a million people to descend on Edinburgh for a mass march to coincide with the G8 summit. He wants them to go there directly from the Live8 concert. <BR><BR><EM>Thanks to our dear friend <STRONG>PlanetBenjamin</STRONG> for bringing this to our attention.</EM></SPAN></P>
</font></div>
	</td>
  </tr>
